---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import "../styles/doaj.scss"
import {Image} from "astro:assets";
import triageOrderPhoto from "../assets/images/doaj/triage-order-photo.png";

const project = {
    client: "Directory of Open Access Journals",
    role: "Product designer",
    contributions: "Product discovery · Requirements definition · Workflow and interaction design · UX/UI design · Accessibility · Client facilitation",
    team: "Cross-functional DOAJ and Cottage Labs team",
    period: "2025 – present",
    status: "Triage design complete · implementation in progress",
};
---

<BaseLayout
        title="DOAJ Editorial Workflow"
        description="Designing a guided, accessible editorial workflow for DOAJ."
>
    <main class="doaj-case-study">
        <article class="case-study">
            <header class="project-hero">
                <p class="project-hero__client">{project.client}</p>

                <h1>
                    Making a complex editorial workflow easier to learn and more efficient to
                    complete
                </h1>

                <p class="project-hero__summary">
                    I am redesigning DOAJ’s editorial workflow: defining how applications move between stages, how
                    reviewers complete and record their work, and how decisions are supported across the process. Triage
                    is the first module I have designed in detail, establishing reusable navigation, interaction and
                    accessibility patterns for the stages that follow.
                </p>

                <dl class="project-meta">
                    <div>
                        <dt>Role</dt>
                        <dd>{project.role}</dd>
                    </div>

                    <div>
                        <dt>Period</dt>
                        <dd>{project.period}</dd>
                    </div>

                    <div>
                        <dt>Status</dt>
                        <dd>{project.status}</dd>
                    </div>

                    <div>
                        <dt>Contributions</dt>
                        <dd>{project.contributions}</dd>
                    </div>

                    <div>
                        <dt>Team</dt>
                        <dd>{project.team}</dd>
                    </div>
                </dl>
            </header>

            <section class="case-study__section">
                <h2>A specialist workflow hidden inside an application form</h2>

                <p>
                    The Directory of Open Access Journals (DOAJ) evaluates applications from publishers to decide
                    whether journals meet its criteria for inclusion. The process involves DOAJ staff and volunteers
                    with different responsibilities, from the initial Triage through further review to the final
                    decision.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The existing system presents reviewers with what is essentially an extended version of the
                    publisher’s application form. It contains the submitted information, but not the editorial work
                    around it: reviewers must know which checks to perform, which external resources to consult and how
                    their findings affect what happens next. Triage has therefore become dependent on one highly
                    experienced specialist, making the work difficult to share and new reviewers difficult to train.
                </p>

                <figure class="case-study-figure old">
                    <div class="image-fade-bottom">
                        <img
                                src="/images/doaj/old.png"
                                width="1288"
                                height="2567"
                                loading="lazy"
                                alt="The existing DOAJ editorial interface, with administrative controls and application sections stacked in one long, continuous form."
                        />
                    </div>
                    <figcaption>
                        And it keeps going...
                    </figcaption>
                </figure>

                <p>
                    DOAJ already had an established visual identity from an earlier service-wide redesign, and a
                    previous UX evaluation had identified problems with the application form. However, the editorial
                    workflow that would address those problems still needed to be designed from the ground up.
                </p>

                <p>
                    I had already worked with DOAJ for several years, contributing to smaller improvements across
                    product design, UI and front-end development. The Editorial Workflow Project became a major
                    workstream within that continuing relationship, developed alongside ongoing maintenance and
                    incremental improvements.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Working closely with DOAJ’s product and editorial teams, I helped translate their standards and
                    working practices into a coherent workflow. I refined and structured the compliance checks,
                    established the guiding design principles and designed the complete Triage experience—from
                    individual interactions to the overview that records the application’s history.
                </p>
            </section>
            <section class="case-study__section">
                <h2>Defining the workflow, not just the interface</h2>

                <p>
                    The project’s initial outline placed UX and UI design after requirements definition. I recommended
                    bringing product design in earlier because decisions about how reviewers would move through checks,
                    record their findings and progress an application were themselves part of defining the requirements.
                    Being involved before those requirements were fixed allowed me to help shape the workflow rather
                    than simply translate a completed specification into screens.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Before our first face-to-face session, I reviewed the existing documentation and earlier UX
                    findings, then created an initial workflow proposal and presentation for the Editorial Team. This
                    made the proposed structure tangible enough for the team to challenge and annotate. During the
                    session, I revised the design and facilitated a short exercise in which we physically arranged the
                    checks around how the Triage specialist completed the work.
                </p>
                <p>
                    The early prototype became a shared requirements tool. Feedback exposed needs that were not yet
                    explicit in the project documentation—for example, recording rejection reasons for reporting,
                    connecting communication with changes of status, and preserving answers and notes across stages.
                    Through weekly meetings, I translated these discussions into revised questions, states, interaction
                    rules and workflows for Triage and the stages that follow.
                </p>
                <figure class="case-study-figure triage-order">
                    <Image src={triageOrderPhoto} format="webp" quality={82}
                           alt="Printed Triage questions arranged into groups and sequences across a table, with handwritten notes added during a face-to-face workshop."
                           class="workshop-photo"/>
                    <figcaption class="board-note">
                        During our workshop in London, we arranged the individual checks into a sequence that reflected
                        how the experienced Triage specialist worked....
                    </figcaption>
                </figure>
            </section>

            <section class="case-study__section">
                <h2>Translating “guided by the system” into design principles</h2>

                <p>
                    One of the client's central requirements was that Editors should be guided by the system through
                    each stage.
                    This described the intended outcome, but did not prescribe how that guidance should work. I
                    translated it
                    into five concrete principles, refined and agreed with the client through our workshops and
                    discussions.
                </p>

                <h3>Present one clear step at a time</h3>
                <p>
                    Break conditional tasks into steps and reveal follow-up fields only when they become relevant,
                    rather than
                    showing every possible path at once.
                </p>

                <h3>
                    Provide the right context for each task
                </h3>
                <p>
                    Place the relevant submitted data, instructions and external resources alongside each check. Keep
                    the
                    journal title, ISSNs and website visible throughout Triage so reviewers can focus without losing the
                    application's identity.
                </p>

                <h3>Make the expected action immediate</h3>
                <p>
                    Make the usual next step the easiest: assign yourself and begin, record an answer and continue, or
                    send the
                    prepared email and reject when rejection is recommended. Keep less common alternatives available
                    without
                    allowing them to compete with the expected path.
                </p>
                <h3>Preserve a complete history</h3>
                <p>
                    Record every answer, note, correction, assignment and communication, including who performed it and
                    when.
                </p>
                <h3>Recommend, but leave the decision with the reviewer</h3>
                <p>
                    Apply the agreed rules consistently and recommend an outcome, while allowing authorised reviewers to
                    override it. The only fixed progression rule is that an application cannot move to another review
                    stage
                    until every Triage check is answered; rejection remains possible at any point.
                </p>
                <p>
                    These principles gave us a shared way to resolve later edge cases without adding arbitrary
                    restrictions. They also established a foundation for the later stages of the editorial workflow.
                </p>

            </section>

            <section class="case-study__section">
                <h2>One question at a time—with freedom to move</h2>
                <figure class="case-study-figure check">
                    <img
                            src="/images/doaj/triage-check.png"
                            width="1903"
                            height="909"
                            loading="lazy"
                            alt="DOAJ Triage design showing an expanded ISSN compliance check with application information, instructions, resources, notes and explicit answer options."
                    />

                    <figcaption>
                        One compliance check brings together the information, guidance and
                        actions needed to complete it.
                    </figcaption>
                </figure>
                <p>
                    The existing interface presents the application as one long, continuous form. Reviewers move
                    backwards and
                    forwards between fields, remember the most efficient order of checks and know for themselves which
                    external
                    resources to consult.
                </p>
                <p>
                    I designed Triage around one compliance check at a time. Each view brings together the criterion,
                    relevant
                    information supplied by the publisher, instructions and reminders, links to required resources, a
                    question-specific note and explicit answer options. Conditional steps appear only when needed. After
                    a
                    straightforward answer, the next unanswered check opens and keyboard focus moves to its heading.
                </p>

                <p>
                    This structure supports reviewers who are learning the process, but it initially concerned the
                    experienced
                    Triage specialist. She could complete the familiar process in approximately seven minutes and
                    worried that
                    moving through individual questions would replace that speed with unnecessary clicking.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Rather than choosing the order in isolation, I facilitated a workshop with the Editorial Team lead
                    and
                    Triage specialist to understand how the review is completed in practice. The specialist explained
                    that when
                    she opens the journal's website or the ISSN Portal, she checks several related criteria while that
                    resource
                    is in front of her. We used this working pattern to group and order the checks, translating her
                    efficient
                    process into a structure that new reviewers can follow while allowing experienced reviewers to work
                    in a
                    familiar way.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The form itself also provides its navigation through a two-level accordion. Expanding a group
                    reveals
                    its
                    short, meaningful question headings; expanding a question reveals the complete check. Reviewers can
                    reach
                    any check within two interactions, work in a different order and then return to the default
                    sequence. Checks
                    they have already answered are skipped automatically.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Answer controls follow a consistent visual and semantic pattern. Compliance, non-compliance,
                    required
                    changes and uncertainty are distinguished through colour, iconography and explicit wording. New
                    reviewers
                    can read the complete answer, while experienced reviewers can recognise recurring actions quickly
                    without
                    relying on colour alone.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Accessibility behaviour is part of the design specification. The prototypes define a consistent
                    heading
                    hierarchy, button semantics and expanded states for the accordion headers, native radio-button
                    behaviour
                    with arrow-key navigation, and focus movement to the next question heading. I annotated the required
                    semantic HTML, ARIA attributes and keyboard interactions for developer reference.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The result is a guided default path without an inflexible wizard: the system can teach the workflow
                    while
                    preserving direct access for people who already know it.
                </p>

            </section>

            <section class="case-study__section">
                <h2>Supporting decisions without making them</h2>

                <p>
                    Completing the checks is only part of Triage. Reviewers must also understand what their answers mean
                    for the
                    application and decide whether to reject it or move it to another stage.
                </p>

                <p>
                    I designed the workflow so that the system interprets the recorded answers and recommends an
                    appropriate
                    outcome. Some non-compliant answers can trigger an immediate rejection recommendation. Others result
                    in
                    rejection only after the complete review, while answers with different severity values contribute to
                    the
                    recommendation for the next stage.
                </p>

                <p>
                    An early version of the requirements assumed that the system would make these decisions
                    automatically.
                    Through discussion, we changed this to a recommendation model: the system applies the agreed rules
                    consistently, but an Editorial reviewer retains authority over the outcome. A reviewer can reject an
                    application at any point, continue after an early rejection recommendation, or override the
                    recommended
                    destination once every check is complete.
                </p>

                <p>
                    This principle also helped us resolve later edge cases. We considered whether the system should
                    prevent
                    progression when every answer was non-compliant. I recommended against introducing a special
                    restriction:
                    blocking one particular combination would immediately raise further questions about where the
                    boundary
                    belonged—for example, whether a single compliant answer should change the result.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Instead, the overview presents every completed check with the same colour, icon and wording used
                    when
                    recording the answer. Unusual patterns remain visible for the Editorial Team to review, while the
                    final
                    decision stays with the authorised user.
                </p>

                <p>
                    When rejection is recommended, the overview presents the suggested outcome alongside editable email
                    content
                    for the publisher. The expected action—sending the email and rejecting the application—is
                    immediately
                    available, while reviewers can still continue the assessment or choose a different outcome.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The result is transparent decision support rather than automated decision-making: the system makes
                    the rules
                    easier to apply without replacing editorial judgement.
                </p>

            </section>
            <section class="case-study__section">
                <h2>Creating one continuous application record</h2>

                <p>
                    The editorial review can pass between several people and stages, so information about the
                    application
                    cannot
                    live only inside individual forms. I designed the overview page as the application's permanent
                    record and
                    control centre.
                </p>

                <p>
                    At any point, reviewers can see who is assigned, read general notes and emails sent through the
                    system, and
                    review the application's history. Each completed stage records its questions, answers,
                    question-specific
                    notes and corrections, together with who performed each action and when.</p>

                <p>
                    The available actions respond to the application's current state. A reviewer might assign the
                    application,
                    begin Triage, transfer responsibility, reject it or complete a recommended outcome. This separation
                    gives
                    each module space to focus on its immediate task while the overview preserves continuity and
                    accountability
                    across the complete process.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Although only Triage has been designed in detail, the same structure is intended to connect the five
                    later
                    stages as they are developed and released.
                </p>

            </section>
            <section class="case-study__section">
                <h2>Designing beyond the first release</h2>

                <p>
                    The detailed design deliberately focuses on Triage, the first module to be implemented and released.
                    However, I designed it as part of a larger editorial process rather than as an isolated form. The
                    shared
                    overview, interaction principles and application history establish a foundation for later stages
                    without
                    prescribing their detailed design prematurely.
                </p>

                <p>
                    I also proposed two longer-term directions for the wider system.
                </p>

                <h3>Preserve the history after acceptance</h3>

                <p>
                    Applications and journals currently remain separate records connected through metadata. I proposed
                    treating
                    an accepted journal as the continuation of its application, allowing administrators to access the
                    complete
                    history from submission through review to inclusion in DOAJ.
                </p>

                <h3>Turn workflow data into operational insight</h3>

                <p>
                    I explored how a redesigned dashboard could use information recorded by the workflow to show how
                    many
                    applications are active at each stage and identify records that may need attention—for example, a
                    Triage
                    review that was started but has remained unfinished.
                </p>

                <p>
                    These concepts had not yet been prioritised at the time of writing. They demonstrate how the Triage
                    design
                    could support the wider service while keeping the immediate delivery focused.
                </p>

            </section>
            <section class="case-study__section">
                <h2>Making branching behaviour buildable</h2>

                <p>
                    Triage contains many possible combinations of answers, follow-up fields and outcomes. Creating one
                    exhaustive high-fidelity prototype would have meant reproducing much of the application's logic
                    before
                    development began.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Instead, we selected representative scenarios and I created interactive walkthroughs of the most
                    important
                    paths. One scenario, for example, shows a reviewer recording a compliant answer to the first check
                    and a
                    non-compliant answer to the second, receiving an early rejection recommendation, and completing the
                    rejection from the overview page.
                </p>

                <p>
                    These scenarios gave the client a concrete way to review and agree the intended behaviour without
                    requiring
                    every possible combination to be prototyped. I also used them when presenting the new design to the
                    wider
                    Editorial Team.
                </p>

                <p>
                    My prototypes included detailed annotations covering state changes, semantic structure, accessible
                    labels,
                    keyboard and focus behaviour, and important edge cases. Working with our QA specialist, I translated
                    the
                    agreed behaviour into functional scenarios and acceptance criteria.
                </p>

                <p>
                    By working closely with the Editorial Team throughout the project, we arrived at an agreed design
                    that
                    reflects how experienced reviewers work while creating a clearer, more supportive path for people
                    learning
                    the process. The interactive scenarios, annotations and acceptance criteria translate that shared
                    solution
                    into an implementation-ready specification for the development and QA teams.
                </p>
            </section>
        </article>
    </main>
</BaseLayout>